{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/d375-measure/packet-v14-2.md'","aggregated_output":"# Packet v14-2\n\n**The question:** Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology permit Alibaba and ByteDance to buy Nvidia RTX PRO 5500 cards by January 31, 2027?\n\nDue 2027-01-31. Possible answers:\n- 1 Yes: MIIT permits Alibaba and ByteDance to buy RTX PRO 5500 cards by January 31, 2027.\n- 2 No: MIIT has not permitted Alibaba and ByteDance to buy RTX PRO 5500 cards by January 31, 2027.\n\n**10 statements read on 2026-09-27:**\n\n- **v14-2-1** (stored attribution: LG Electronics): LG plans to pursue more megawatt-scale AI data center projects in North America, targeting hyperscalers and colocation operators; the item gives no deadline or project amount.\n  - Passage: \"With its NPN status, LG plans to pursue more megawatt-scale AI data center projects, particularly in North America, targeting hyperscalers and colocation operators.\"\n- **v14-2-2** (stored attribution: NVIDIA management): NVIDIA management expected gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\n  - Passage: \"Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%.\"\n- **v14-2-3** (stored attribution: Zacks.com): Zacks said analysts were consistently raising their estimates for Nvidia and Micron, describing their outlooks as steadily improving.\n  - Passage: \"both companies are enjoying a steadily improving outlook, with analysts consistently raising their estimates.\"\n- **v14-2-4** (stored attribution: NVIDIA management): NVIDIA management said its preliminary fiscal 2028 revenue-growth outlook was supply-constrained.\n  - Passage: \"Management called that outlook supply-constrained.\"\n- **v14-2-5** (stored attribution: Nvidia and SpaceX; Elon Musk for the system design and deployment plan): Nvidia and SpaceX announced a partnership to develop GPUs for SpaceX's planned orbital data centers; Musk said they had designed a space-optimized Vera Rubin NVL72 system, which SpaceX planned to launch into orbit toward the end of 2027 and begin scaling in 2028.\n  - Passage: \"In addition to helping expand xAI's computing capacity, Nvidia and SpaceX also announced a partnership to develop GPUs for the company's planned future orbital data centers. Cosmic radiation can affect current chips, flipping bits from a 0 to a 1 or vice versa, causing significant computational errors. Worst of all, there is currently no way to flip it back, and it's difficult to detect without a redundant system to check it against. In a post on X, Musk said the two companies have designed a Vera Rubin NVL72 system optimized for use in space. It plans to launch the system into orbit toward the end of 2027 and then start scaling it in 2028.\"\n- **v14-2-6** (stored attribution: Zacks.com): Zacks expected Nvidia’s Q3 earnings to rise 90% year over year and revenue to rise 91.2%.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia's Q3 earnings are expected to increase +90% year-over-year on +91.2% higher revenues\"\n- **v14-2-7** (stored attribution: The Motley Fool author): The author estimated that 1 GW of computing power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, including about $35 billion of Nvidia chips and other components.\n  - Passage: \"One gigawatt of compute power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, with chips and other Nvidia components making up about $35 billion of that total cost.\"\n- **v14-2-8** (stored attribution: The Information): China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology reportedly asked domestic firms including Alibaba and ByteDance for planned purchase quantities and intended uses for Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 GPUs.\n  - Passage: \"The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would use them for.\"\n- **v14-2-9** (stored attribution: LG Electronics): LG Electronics joined NVIDIA’s power and cooling partner network for data centers; the item gives no date for the announcement.\n  - Passage: \"LG Electronics has joined Nvidia's partner network for the U.S. AI company's power and cooling business for data centers, the Korean company said Sunday.\"\n- **v14-2-10** (stored attribution: LG Electronics): LG’s 1-megawatt coolant distribution unit completed Nvidia qualification; the item gives no date.\n  - Passage: \"Its 1-megawatt CDU subsequently completed Nvidia qualification as well.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":\"v14-2-1\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"LG's North American data center plans do not address the Chinese purchase decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-2\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Nvidia's gross margin forecast does not address the permit decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-3\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Analysts' general estimates do not address the permit decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-4\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Nvidia's revenue outlook does not address permission to purchase these cards.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-5\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The SpaceX partnership concerns a different system and deployment plan.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-6\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Nvidia's earnings and revenue forecasts do not address the permit decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-7\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The cost of computing power does not address permission to purchase these cards.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-8\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"MIIT's reported inquiry concerns the same companies and cards involved in the decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-9\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"LG's data center partnership does not address the permit decision.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v14-2-10\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Qualification of LG's cooling equipment does not address the permit decision.\"}\n{\"done\":true,\"items\":10}"}}
